A||||3||William||Removing this %^$#@$ Grant steering wheel||||1||
Z||000000||William||01-25-2001||04:03 PM||||I just pulled the stock wheel, with a puller rented from Autozone, and installed a Grant wheel. Turns out I was off one spline and will need to remove and repostion the wheel. This Grant hub appears to require a 3 hole puller but the autozone unit only works with 2 hole hubs. All the steering wheel pullers that I see for sale, are the 2 hole type. Grant makes the right  one for thier wheels but I don't remeber seeing it at auto parts store. What am I to do now? Help!||||reg||1
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||01-25-2001||04:13 PM||||William a flange puller also known as harmonic puller, timming gear puller<BR> Auto zone should have one<P>Runs about $30.00 for a good one????<BR>hope this helps<P>DXR_DAD||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-25-2001||07:18 PM||||I remember the grant wheel adaptors having a 4th hole. you use that ant one of the wheel anchor holes to put the adaptor. If they don't have the 4th hole then if there is enough metal you could drill and tap one easy enough.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||01-25-2001||10:06 PM||||I ran into this problem too.  I put the bolts in two of the three holes with the "piston" portion of the puller backed all the way off.  Then I just placed a piece of wood between the puller and end of the steering shaft.<P>Then I slowly applied pressure making sure I wasn't bending anything.  If it won't budge try switching a bolt to the third hole and pull some more.  Main thing is to hold up against the block of wood to keep the pressure going as normal to the steering shaft as possible.<P>Hope this works if you're in a jam, did for me.  I'd recommend the 3-way puller if possible tho.||||reg||1
